    "IFS sessions in Palo Alto work directly with the perfectionist, the inner critic, the driven part, and the parts underneath them that those parts have been protecting -- often for decades. Getting curious about the critic rather than arguing with it. Understanding what the perfectionist is afraid will happen if the standards slip. Building a relationship with the exhausted part that has been waiting for permission to rest. For Palo Alto clients who have tried to manage these parts through cognitive reframing and self-compassion practices, the direct engagement of IFS often produces a qualitatively different experience within the first few sessions.",
    "Progress in IFS for Palo Alto clients tends to be gradual and then significant -- a period of mapping the internal landscape and building relationships with specific parts, followed by shifts that feel structural rather than symptomatic. The inner critic does not disappear but its activity reduces when its underlying concerns are addressed. The perfectionist does not stop caring about quality but it stops generating the constant pressure that makes quality feel like a survival requirement. Most clients notice meaningful change within 12 to 20 sessions. The work is experiential rather than analytical -- which is precisely why it reaches what Palo Alto's intellectually rigorous population has been unable to think its way to.",
